---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')
const fig = (n: string) => `${base}/figures/${n}`
const vid = (n: string) => `${base}/videos/${n}`

const authors = [
	{ name: 'Minkyu Kang', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/' },
	{ name: 'JinHo Kim' },
	{ name: 'Eunseop Shin' },
	{ name: 'Dayeon Hwang' },
]

const links = [
	{ label: 'Project Weasel', href: 'https://mk040412.github.io/Project-Weasel/' },
	{ label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA' },
	{ label: 'Slides', href: 'https://github.com/MK040412/Mean-Collapse-VLA/blob/main/README.md' },
]
---

<Layout
	title='Mean Collapse is a Serious Bottleneck of VLA'
	pageTitle='Mean Collapse in VLA'
	description='When VLA models act on the latest observation only, aliased states collapse the policy to a mean action — a small memory-based steering module fixes it without retraining the base VLA.'
	authors={authors}
	links={links}
	hero={fig('steering_module.png')}
	heroAlt='Memory-based steering module added to a frozen VLA'
	faviconHref={`${base}/weasel-logo.png`}
>
	<div class='highlight-box'>
		<h2>TL;DR</h2>
		<p>
			A VLA trained to act on the <b>latest observation only</b> (Markovian policy
			<i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>)) collapses on POMDP tasks: when two
			distinct underlying states share a similar observation, the optimal Markovian action is the
			<b>mean over plausible futures</b> — and the mean is often wrong. We show this on an
			Ant-Maze POMDP variant: success drops from <b>0.53 → 0.16</b> (a <b>3.3×</b> drop) when the
			top-view is hidden and only the 3rd-person view is available.
		</p>
		<p>
			<b>Memory exists, but memory is not enough on its own.</b> The fix that actually works in
			our setup is a <b>memory-based steering module</b> — a small SSM + Perceiver head that
			builds a fixed-size memory <i>Z</i> from past ViT features and injects it back into the
			frozen base VLA. With steering on the 3rd-person view, success jumps to <b>0.84</b>,
			surpassing even the MDP top-view baseline.
		</p>
		<p>
			Same pathology shows up beyond robotics: a frozen GUI agent on Duolingo gets stuck in a
			loop because solved and fresh buttons look identical — pure observation-conditioning
			cannot tell them apart.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h1>Background — MDP vs. POMDP</h1>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('mdp_vs_pomdp.png')} alt='MDP vs POMDP graphical models' />
		<figcaption>
			MDP agent acts on the state <i>s</i> directly; the POMDP agent maintains a belief
			<i>b</i> from observations <i>o</i>.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>MDP:</b> <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>) — the agent sees the
			task-relevant state directly.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>POMDP:</b> <i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>h<sub>t</sub></i>) where
			<i>h<sub>t</sub></i> = (<i>o<sub>0</sub>, a<sub>0</sub>, …, o<sub>t</sub></i>) — only
			noisy/partial observations are available, so the agent must condition on history.
		</li>
		<li>
			A Markovian policy on <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> is <b>suboptimal</b> when
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> ↛ <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h1>Most VLA Benchmarks Hide the POMDP</h1>
	<p>
		Most VLA benchmarks are effectively MDPs: the current scene already encodes the task-relevant
		state, so a policy reading only the latest observation looks fine. LIBERO and SIMPLER-style
		manipulation are like that — the relevant scene is on screen at every timestep.
	</p>
	<p>
		GUI agents (UI-TARS-style) are typically deployed the same way: each action is decided from
		a <b>summary + the current screenshot only</b>. Both setups assume
		<i>π</i>(<i>a<sub>t</sub></i> | <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>) suffices — i.e. Markovian on the latest
		observation.
	</p>

	<h1>But Real Tasks Are POMDPs</h1>
	<p>
		Crafting a pickaxe in Minecraft needs the fact &ldquo;I already chopped a tree&rdquo; — and
		that fact is not in the current screen. The current observation <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> shows
		only the scene ahead; inventory and past actions are <b>hidden state</b>. Real long-horizon
		tasks (Minecraft, GUI agents, manipulation under occlusion) are POMDPs: the policy needs
		<b>history</b> <i>h<sub>t</sub></i>, not just <i>o<sub>t</sub></i>.
	</p>

	<h1>Setup — A Controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant Maze</h1>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('maze_setup.png')} alt='OGBench maze setup with two views' />
		<figcaption>
			We build a controllable POMDP in OGBench Ant Maze. The agent sees only the
			<b>3rd-person view (left)</b>; the <b>top-view (right)</b> is the hidden ground-truth
			state.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('two_views.png')} alt='Same world, two views — visual aliasing' />
		<figcaption>
			Top-view: <i>o<sub>t</sub></i> = <i>s<sub>t</sub></i> (MDP). 3rd-person:
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> ∼ Ω(· | <i>s<sub>t</sub></i>) (POMDP). Visually similar 3rd-person
			states can correspond to <b>different</b> next steps — the source of mean collapse.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<p>
		<b>Which view does the agent receive?</b> The <b>3rd-person view</b> is the policy
		input; the top-view is used only to define the MDP baseline.
	</p>

	<h1>Method — Ant VLA, OpenVLA-OFT Style</h1>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('ant_vla_training.png')} alt='Ant VLA training pipeline' />
		<figcaption>
			Backbone: <b>Qwen3-VL 2B</b>, trained in the OpenVLA-OFT style. Both the ViT and the LLM
			decode head are fine-tuned with <b>LoRA</b>. Input is the 3rd-person observation plus the
			Ant proprioception and a <code>Go to the point ⟨x, y⟩</code> instruction; output is a
			parallel-decoded action chunk.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('qwen3vl_arch.png')} alt='Qwen3-VL backbone architecture (from DASH)' />
		<figcaption>Qwen3-VL backbone — diagram reused from the DASH report.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Result — Hiding the Top-View Collapses the Policy</h1>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('result_baseline.png')} alt='Bar chart: top-view 0.53 vs 3rd-person 0.16' />
		<figcaption>
			Same policy, same training, only the input view changes. <b>Top-view (MDP): 0.53</b>;
			<b>3rd-person (POMDP): 0.16</b>. <b>1500 rollouts</b>, maze: medium.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		Below: success vs failure rollouts on the 3rd-person POMDP setup. The same Markovian policy
		intermittently solves a maze and intermittently <b>loops or stalls</b> on visually aliased
		corridors — exactly the mean-collapse signature.
	</p>

	<div class='video-grid'>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('success_00.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>Success — 3rd-person, no memory.</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('success_03.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>Success — 3rd-person, no memory.</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('fail_00.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>Failure — agent loops on visually aliased state.</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<figure>
			<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('fail_01.mp4')}></video>
			<figcaption>Failure — wrong branch chosen at an aliased junction.</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<h1>Fix — A Memory-Based Steering Module on the Frozen VLA</h1>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('steering_module.png')} alt='Steering module added to the frozen VLA' />
		<figcaption>
			Learnable steering (peach) builds a fixed-size memory <i>Z</i> from past ViT features via
			a <b>GLA temporal mixer (linear SSM)</b> + <b>Perceiver compression</b>, then injects it
			into the frozen ViT at L8 / L16 and into the action head through gated residuals. The
			<b>frozen base VLA (green) is never re-trained</b>.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h2>Formulation</h2>
	<p>Past ViT features <i>h<sup>(ℓ)</sup><sub>t−k</sub></i> at depths ℓ ∈ &#123;8, 16&#125;, k = 1, …, K:</p>
	<pre class='codebox'><code>{`(1) Linear SSM mix — fixed-size state, content-gated decay:
    S_k = σ(g_k) ⊙ S_{k-1} + K_k^T V_k
    O_k = Q_k S_k

(2) Compress to m = 32 memory tokens (Perceiver cross-attention):
    Z^(ℓ) = CrossAttn(Q_learn, O_{1:K}^(ℓ))   ∈ R^{32×d}

(3) Gated residual injection into frozen ViT:
    h~_t^(ℓ) = h_t^(ℓ) + σ(α_ℓ) · CrossAttn(h_t^(ℓ), Z^(ℓ))

(4) Residual correction on frozen action head:
    a_t = π_base(o_t)  +  σ(α_res) · Δ(Z^(16))
                  ↑frozen↑`}</code></pre>

	<h2>Why a State-Space Model?</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<b>Long token sequence.</b> Flattened past is <i>T = K · N<sub>patch</sub></i> ∼ 2000
			tokens. Quadratic attention is <i>O(T²)</i>; linear SSM is <i>O(T)</i> with a fixed-size
			state <i>S</i>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Content-gated decay.</b> σ(<i>g<sub>t</sub></i>) learns a per-token forget — more
			expressive than RetNet&apos;s fixed γ, more stable than plain linear attention.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Length extrapolation.</b> Train with <i>K</i> ∼ 𝒰[1, 32] + sinusoidal temporal PE →
			robust at <i>K</i> = (64, 128) at inference.
		</li>
		<li>
			<b>Identity-init gates.</b> σ(α) = 0 → the module starts as a no-op, recovers the base VLA
			exactly, then learns corrections safely.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h1>Result — Steering Recovers Lost Performance</h1>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('result_steering.png')} alt='Bar chart: top-view 0.53 / 3rd-person 0.16 / 3rd-person + Steering 0.84' />
		<figcaption>
			On the same 3rd-person input that previously collapsed to <b>0.16</b>, adding the
			memory-based steering module pushes success to <b>0.84</b> — past <b>even the MDP
			top-view baseline (0.53)</b>. The frozen base VLA is unchanged; only the small steering
			module is trained.
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<p>
		<i>
			SSM = cheap + long + stable — the right tool to absorb <i>h<sub>t</sub></i> into a frozen
			VLA.
		</i>
	</p>

	<h1>Bonus — Same Pathology on a GUI Agent (Duolingo Loop-Case)</h1>
	<p>
		The exact same Markovian-on-<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> failure shows up in a real GUI agent.
		<b>GELab-Zero-4B-preview</b>, a VLM-based agent, runs on a Duolingo &ldquo;tap the matching
		pairs&rdquo; quiz. Solved pairs turn darker, but solved and fresh buttons still look almost
		identical — and the agent has no memory of which ones it already tapped.
	</p>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<img src={fig('gui_loop.png')} alt='GUI agent loop case on Duolingo' />
		<figcaption>
			The agent re-taps a solved pair, the app rejects it, and the screen returns to the same
			state — <i>C → E → C → E …</i>. Same pathology as the Ant POMDP:
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> (screenshot) ↛ <i>s<sub>t</sub></i> (solved-set).
		</figcaption>
	</figure>
	<figure class='figure-block'>
		<video controls muted loop playsinline preload='metadata' src={vid('gui_rollout.mp4')}></video>
		<figcaption>GUI agent rollout (sped up) — getting stuck in the loop.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<h1>Conclusion</h1>
	<ul>
		<li>
			Ant Maze and the GUI Loop-Case fail the <b>same way</b>: a policy on
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i> loops because the past is invisible — that is mean collapse on
			aliased states.
		</li>
		<li>
			The fix is <b>history</b>: condition on
			<i>h<sub>t</sub></i> = (<i>o<sub>0:t</sub>, a<sub>0:t−1</sub></i>), not just
			<i>o<sub>t</sub></i>.
		</li>
		<li>
			Memory alone is not the whole story; what restored performance here was a <b>memory-based
			steering module</b> (linear SSM + Perceiver) injected into the frozen VLA at low cost.
		</li>
		<li>
			Result: a memory-augmented VLA on the same backbone, POMDP-ready, without re-training the
			base policy.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<hr />
	<h1>Citation</h1>
	<pre class='codebox'><code>{`@misc{kang2026meancollapse,
  title={Mean Collapse is a Serious Bottleneck of VLA},
  author={Kang, Minkyu and Kim, JinHo and Shin, Eunseop and Hwang, Dayeon},
  year={2026},
  howpublished={\\url{https://mk040412.github.io/Mean-Collapse-VLA/}},
  note={Team Weasel}
}`}</code></pre>

